How this suite was run (it is designed for a THROWAWAY container, not your host):

  # 1. get a real minimal Arch rootfs
  curl -LO https://geo.mirror.pkgbuild.com/iso/latest/archlinux-bootstrap-x86_64.tar.zst
  sudo tar -xf archlinux-bootstrap-x86_64.tar.zst          # -> root.x86_64/

  # 2. prepare it (mirror, keyring, prerequisites, a normal user)
  sudo systemd-nspawn -D root.x86_64 --as-pid2 /bin/bash -lc '
    echo "Server = https://geo.mirror.pkgbuild.com/\$repo/os/\$arch" > /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist
    pacman-key --init && pacman-key --populate archlinux && pacman -Sy --noconfirm
    pacman -S --needed --noconfirm sudo git python gum
    useradd -m -G wheel tester
    echo "%wheel ALL=(ALL:ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL" > /etc/sudoers.d/wheel'

  # 3. copy the repo + suite in and run as the unprivileged user
  sudo cp -r <repo> root.x86_64/home/tester/repo3
  sudo cp tests/fresh-arch-suite.sh root.x86_64/home/tester/suite.sh
  sudo systemd-nspawn -D root.x86_64 --as-pid2 /bin/bash -lc \
    'chown -R tester:tester /home/tester; su - tester -c "bash ~/suite.sh"'

Known container caveats (NOT bugs in the scripts):
  - nspawn mounts the rootfs nosuid, so `sudo` cannot elevate. Root-guard
    checks are therefore skipped in the user run and verified in a root run.
  - /proc/sys is read-only and udev is not running, so `sysctl --system` and
    `udevadm` fail. The tweaks treat those reloads as best-effort for exactly
    this reason; the config files still install correctly.
  - nspawn shares the host's /sys, so USB hardware detection sees the HOST's
    devices. The Bolt check asserts agreement with sysfs, not a fixed answer.
